WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.280
Helium is not a renewable resource,
at least not on any human timescale.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:08.100
It's not a fossil fuel, but it is
made very slowly by radioactive decay

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.240
deep down in the ground.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.650
So in terms of managing it, we should
be treating it like a fossil fuel.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.980
Once it's pulled up from the ground, used,
and dispersed into the atmosphere,

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.230
it is ludicrously expensive to recover.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:23.910
And over time, that dispersed helium is
light enough and unreactive enough that

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.760
eventually, very, very slowly,
it escapes off into space,

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.680
which is a problem
because helium is enormously useful.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.840
We use it in all sorts
of science and medicine,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.320
to keep the magnets in MRI machines cool,
in manufacturing processes,

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.642
and, yes, to inflate
blimps and party balloons.

00:00:40.642 --> 00:00:42.200
And over the last decade or so,

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.280
there have been a lot of
breathless news articles

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.600
proclaiming that the world
is running out of helium.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.700
A lot of the world's helium comes from
under the Great Plains of the USA.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.250
And here in Amarillo, Texas,
the US government

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.070
has the National Helium Reserve.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.780
At least, they do for now.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:00:59.980
- We're standing on top of the
Bush Dome,

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.140
the reservoir that acts as the
storage reservoir,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:07.620
which is approximately 3,500 feet
underground.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:12.180
As a sponge absorbs water and
holds that water in the sponge,

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.940
similarly, the small pores in the rock
are spaces where the helium can reside.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.090
It's solid rock to the eye,
but it is porous

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:24.390
and allows the gas to be stored
very efficiently underground.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.120
This reservoir was a
natural gas reservoir.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.800
It was depleted, then recharged
in the sixties

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.470
with 32 billion cubic feet of helium
into the ground.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.830
So it's actually helium that
we've injected previously

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.270
that we're extracting now.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:43.810
The whole program was to conserve
the helium for about 100 years

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.260
of the consumption rate then.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.320
Of course, helium has become such a
popular element, it has so many uses,

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.280
that now, that rate is quite a bit more
than originally estimated.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:58.830
That helium has been withdrawn
over approximately 15 years.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.200
And we are now reaching
the end of our program

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.900
as the field is declining in pressure.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.990
Helium is notoriously known for being
able to escape through any small opening.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.380
It's used as a leak detection substance.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.150
You can detect helium very easily.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:16.500
And if it's able to get out, you have a hole
somewhere in that device or product.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.170
By putting it back in the ground,
it's not leaking out

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.970
of a stainless steel
container or a big truck,

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.293
it's easily monitored and controlled.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.840
- The plant here is in its annual
week of maintenance right now,

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.790
which is one of the reasons
I'm allowed to get quite this close.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.520
There are still parts I'm not
allowed to show you, though.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.610
- The equipment behind me is
actually an enrichment unit.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.520
All that gas that was stored in
the ground has mixed and blended

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.713
with natural gas from the field, and
it's no longer in an enriched state.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.420
So by bringing it through the equipment,
we take that gas back up to

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:52.180
approximately 80% purity of helium,
about 20% impurity of nitrogen.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:56.800
That's what we produce and send up
our 425-mile pipeline

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.660
to the private helium plants
that then take it to 100%.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.540
There are places in the world that are
running out of helium, that's correct.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:07.520
But there's still approximately
80 to 100 years' worth

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.740
of known helium reserves
in the United States, in Wyoming.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:16.590
So to say that we're running out of
helium is actually a little bit incorrect.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:21.420
There's still a lot of helium available
and new helium sources being found.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:24.570
With the recent increases
in the helium price,

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:28.890
that's actually stimulated industry
to go look for more helium resources.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.000
And they have found more.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.900
So we expect that
in the next few years,

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.040
more of this helium
will be brought to market.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.570
And the shortage will be alleviated,
at least for a short period of time.

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:42.210
- Running out of something on
our current planetary scale

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.410
doesn't mean there suddenly
won't be any at all.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:48.180
It just means that the price of what's left
will rise higher and higher and higher

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.320
until the lower supply meets
lower demand,

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.540
which might make some more
difficult to reach reservoirs of helium

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:55.560
under the ground worth exploring.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.090
That's capitalism.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.400
Because of all that uncertainty,
the price of helium

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.740
has been fluctuating a lot
over the last few years.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.160
And researchers are often having
trouble finding enough money

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.760
to keep their experiments going.

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.800
And yes, it's why filling party balloons
is getting really expensive.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:13.800
- We are now in the final
two years of our program

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:17.160
and facing the last part of
our privatization effort.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:22.680
By the year 2021, we will be
stepping out of the helium activity

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.410
and transferring it to private entities.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:28.680
Surprisingly enough, the question
comes up about whether or not

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.660
we should be using party balloons.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.060
I happen to be a real
fan of party balloons.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.960
So for my preference, I still am glad
that we have party balloons.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.180
With the price of helium where it is,

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:41.200
there's so much conservation going on now

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.340
that the party balloons are actually
a very small part of the market.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:47.850
We've seen now in recent years
with the newer helium sources

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:51.600
coming online and this great
effort towards helium conservation

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.410
that that's really had an impact in
making more helium available for everyone.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.410
So I think the party balloons
kind of got a bad rap.

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:03.060
- I did think about ending this video by
delivering my conclusion in a silly voice

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.710
by breathing from a helium balloon, but
honestly, we do need to conserve helium

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.900
and I figured that
would set a bad example.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:11.473
So this...

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.620
...it's just regular air.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:22.020
I'm glad that flew away! That would've
looked terrible if it hadn't worked.

